For every nonempty finite simple graph $G$, the function $F_G$ attains its maximum. Every maximizer $w$ has positive coordinates and satisfies $$\label{eq:stationarity}
 \log(1/w_v)=w(N_G(v)) \qquad(v\in V(G)).$$ In particular, $w_v\le1$. For every nonnegative vector $q$, $$\label{eq:optimizer-divergence}
 0\le F_G(w)-F_G(q)
 =\sum_v\left(q_v\log\frac{q_v}{w_v}-q_v+w_v\right)
   +M_G(q-w).$$ Here a term with $q_v=0$ in the sum equals $w_v$.

Let $r\ge2$ be an integer and let $w$ maximize $F_G$ on a nonempty finite $K_r$-free graph $G$. For every $x\ge1$ and all vertex sets $S,D$ with $w(S),w(D)\le e^x$, $$\label{eq:optimizer-cross-mass}
 e_w(S,D)\le C_r g_x(w(S)),
 \qquad C_r=16r^2.$$ The sets $S$ and $D$ need not be disjoint.

For a positive-weighted finite simple graph $G$, put $$s_u=w(N_G(u)),\qquad
 c_{uv}=w(N_G(u)\cap N_G(v)),\qquad
 h_u=\sum_{v\in N_G(u)}w_vc_{uv}.$$ Thus $h_u=2M_{G[N_G(u)]}(w)$ is twice the edge mass inside the neighborhood of $u$. Then $$\label{eq:triangle-normalizations}
 \sum_{\{u,v\}\in E(G)}w_uw_vc_{uv}=3T_G(w),
 \qquad
 \sum_{u\in V(G)}w_uh_u=6T_G(w).$$ In particular, if every neighborhood has weight at most $L$, then $$\label{eq:terminal-triangle-bound}
 T_G(w)\le \frac{L}{3}M_G(w).$$*

*For every $\tau>0$, there is a spanning subgraph $G'$ obtained by successive edge deletions such that every edge of $G'$ has common-neighbor weight at least $\tau$ and $$\label{eq:peeling-loss}
 T_{G'}(w)\ge T_G(w)-\tau M_G(w).$$ For each nonisolated vertex of $G'$, its neighborhood and common-neighbor weights, computed in $G'$, satisfy $$s_u\ge\tau,\qquad h_u\ge\tau s_u.$$ If $G$ satisfies <a href="#eq:cross-mass" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:cross-mass">[eq:cross-mass]</a>, then so does $G'$, with the same constant.

Let $I$ and $J$ be finite nonempty sets, and let $(p_i)_{i\in I}$ be probability distributions on $J$. There are jointly distributed random variables $(Y_i)_{i\in I}$ such that $Y_i$ has law $p_i$ and, for every $i,i'\in I$, $$\mathbb P(Y_i\ne Y_{i'})
 \le \frac{2\|p_i-p_{i'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}}
 {1+\|p_i-p_{i'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}}
 \le 2\|p_i-p_{i'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.$$ The resulting joint law has finite support.

There is a choice of these labels for which the projection $(u,y)\mapsto u$ preserves weights, maps directed edges injectively to directed edges of $H$, and satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 \max_{a\in V(H')}w(N_{H'}(a))&\le e^{\sqrt x},
 \label{eq:split-neighborhood}\\
 T_{H'}(w)&\ge(1-a_C\log x/\sqrt x)T_H(w),
 \label{eq:split-triangles}
\end{aligned}$$ where $a_C=6(B_C+A_C+1)$.

Let $G$ be a finite positive-vertex-weighted graph satisfying the cross-mass condition <a href="#eq:cross-mass" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:cross-mass">[eq:cross-mass]</a> with constant $C\ge1$, and suppose that $\max_vw(N_G(v))\le e^x$ for some $x>X(C)$. There is a finite positive-vertex-weighted graph $G'$ with a weight-preserving map to $G$ that is injective on directed edges, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \max_vw(N_{G'}(v))&\le e^{\sqrt x},\label{eq:step-neighborhood}\\
 T_{G'}(w)&\ge
 \left(1-a_C\frac{\log x}{\sqrt x}\right)T_G(w)
       -\frac{M_G(w)}x.\label{eq:step-triangles}
\end{aligned}$$ It also satisfies the same cross-mass condition, and $M_{G'}(w)\le M_G(w)$.

For every integer $r\ge4$, every real number $\Delta\ge3$, and every finite $K_r$-free graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and maximum degree at most $\Delta$, $$\alpha(G)\ge \frac{F_G^*}{D_r\log\Delta}
 \qquad\text{and}\qquad
 \alpha(G)\ge \frac{n\log\Delta}{4D_r\Delta}.$$
